course: dictionary
lesson: 3557
title: The femur, the thigh bone
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The femur is your thigh bone. It is the long bone that runs from your hip down to your knee. «The femur, or thigh bone, is the longest and strongest bone in the body.» When you see femur, picture the bone in the top part of your leg.
explain: Context helps you work out the meaning. «Your legs carry your whole weight all day, so their bones are the biggest you have.» The clues are weight and biggest, so the femur must be a large, strong bone in the leg. Check by swapping in your meaning: «The thigh bone is the longest and strongest bone in the body» still makes sense.
explain: Science texts often put two names together. «The longest bone in the body is the thigh bone (femur).» The words in brackets give the scientific name, and it means the same thing as the words before it.
fact: The femur is the thigh bone, between the hip and the knee.
fact: The femur is the longest and strongest bone in the human body.
fact: The femur is about a quarter of a person's height.
fact: The smallest bone is the stirrup bone in the middle ear, about 3 millimetres long.
fact: Scientists study fossil femurs of dinosaurs to learn how big the dinosaurs were.
exercise: «The femur, or thigh bone, is the longest and strongest bone in the body.» Which bone is the femur? | *The thigh bone, between the hip and the knee | The bone in the upper arm, between the shoulder and the elbow | The stirrup bone in the middle ear
exercise: Mia says the femur is the smallest bone in the body. Is she right? | Yes, it is about the size of a grain of rice | *No, the stirrup bone is the smallest, and the femur is the longest | No, the femur is in the neck | Yes, it is tiny and light
exercise: Find and copy the word that means the thigh bone: «The longest bone in the body is the thigh bone (femur), about a quarter of your height.» | height | longest | *femur | quarter
test: About how tall is the femur compared with a person? | About a tenth of their height | About the same as their height | About twice their height | *About a quarter of their height
test: Where is the femur? | Between the elbow and the wrist | *Between the hip and the knee | In the middle ear | Inside the skull
exam: Why do scientists study fossil femurs of dinosaurs? | To find out what the dinosaurs ate for lunch | To prove that dinosaurs could fly | *To find out how big the dinosaurs were | To learn how the dinosaurs got their names
